{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent negative bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices across most horizons (often by ~2–10 EUR), especially during uptrends/rallies.",
    "Trend lag/underreaction: after a breakout (e.g., history rising into mid-70s), the model projects flattening/soft pullbacks while the market continues trending higher for ~1–3 weeks.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: error tends to grow with lead time; 10–25 day horizons show the largest shortfall (model stays ~72–76 while outcomes print ~80–84).",
    "False mean-reversion: model repeatedly pulls forecasts back toward ~72–75 even when realized regime shifts upward; apply regime-detection (if last 5–10 days are strongly positive, reduce mean-reversion strength).",
    "Late-horizon reversal bias: in examples 2–3 the model turns sharply bearish in the last ~5–10 forecast days (down to high-60s/low-70s) while outcomes remain high-70s/low-80s; dampen late-horizon downswings unless confirmed by recent downside momentum.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast path is smoother than reality; during strong rallies, add an upside convexity/volatility adjustment (wider bands, higher drift) rather than a flat trajectory."
  ]
}
